INT. CLOSED MUSEUM OF CLOCKS, GALLERY - NIGHT

Hundreds of clocks, all stopped at different hours. Glass cases. Brass gears under spotlights. A wall of cuckoo clocks, beaks open, silent.

DETECTIVE RUTH OKONJO-HALVORSEN (40s, wool coat, flashlight) crouches over a smashed display case. Glass crunches under her boots. She picks up a pendulum, turns it over, sets it down.

Footsteps behind her. She doesn't turn.

RUTH
You're late, Marguerite.

A WOMAN steps out from behind a longcase clock: MARGUERITE DELACROIX-ASANTE (40s, gloves, raincoat, a museum badge on a lanyard).

MARGUERITE
I was told eleven.

RUTH
It's eleven-twenty.

MARGUERITE
My watch is slow.

RUTH
Everything in this building is slow.

Marguerite's mouth twitches. She walks to the case, careful not to touch the glass.

MARGUERITE
What's missing?

RUTH
The Harrison.

MARGUERITE
The Harrison.

RUTH
The chronometer. Seventeen sixty-one.

MARGUERITE
Ruth, that's worth--

RUTH
More than this city. I know.

MARGUERITE
How did they get in?

RUTH
Through the cuckoo wall.

MARGUERITE
That's impossible. It's a hundred pounds of timber and a steel frame.

RUTH
Somebody had the key.

MARGUERITE
Who?

RUTH
You tell me.

Marguerite stiffens. The nearest clock ticks once, as if the sound were a mistake, and falls silent again.

MARGUERITE
You think I did it.

RUTH
I think you're the curator.

MARGUERITE
Ruth.

RUTH
I think you carry the only key.

MARGUERITE
I lost it Tuesday.

RUTH
Where?

MARGUERITE
In the carpark.

RUTH
You didn't report it.

MARGUERITE
I thought I'd find it.

RUTH
Marguerite, you are the least careless person I have ever met.

Marguerite takes off her gloves one finger at a time. Lays them on the case.

MARGUERITE
I didn't take it.

RUTH
I didn't say you did.

MARGUERITE
You've been saying it for twenty minutes.

RUTH
I've been listening for twenty minutes. There's a difference.

A new sound: a faint, mechanical whir. Both women freeze. The cuckoo wall, a hundred painted doors, shivers. One by one, they pop open.

MARGUERITE
(whispering)
That's not on a timer.

RUTH
No.

A hundred cuckoos emerge on little sprung arms, each with its beak wide, and begin to chime together, wildly out of tune. The noise fills the gallery like a swarm.

Ruth draws her sidearm. Marguerite grabs her arm.

MARGUERITE
Don't! You'll hit the glass!

RUTH
Then who's doing this?

MARGUERITE
It's the mainspring! Someone's wound it!

RUTH
Where?

Marguerite points. Behind the cuckoos, in the shadow, a figure steps out: a MAN IN A GRAY WORK COAT (60s, clock-repairer's loupe pushed up on his forehead, the chronometer in his hand like a baby).

MAN
It's not stolen.

RUTH
Put it down.

MAN
It's mine.

MARGUERITE
Henri?

The man looks at her. His lip trembles.

HENRI
It was my father's. They took it. Fifty years ago.

Ruth lowers the gun. The cuckoos chime on. Marguerite crosses the glass-strewn floor, slowly, and stops an arm's length from him.

MARGUERITE
(gently)
Henri. Give it to me.

HENRI
You'll lock it up again.

MARGUERITE
I'll make sure it's in the right case.

He studies her face for a long time. Then, as the last cuckoo clicks back into its door and the gallery falls silent, he lays the chronometer in her gloved hands.
